          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A surprising parallel between classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            and the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+representations"} Galois representations
            was noted in the beginning of the 60’s; shortly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Tate"} Tate
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            attempted to understand the topology of p-adic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} varieties
            in light of this analogy. A series of precise conjectures about p-adic Hodge theory was formulated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marc+Fontaine"} Fontaine
            at the beginning of the 80’s; three different proofs were proposed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerd+Faltings"} Faltings
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Tsuji
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Takeshi+Tsuji"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"} Beilinson
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bhargav+Bhatt"} Bhatt
          %p
            %em
              This paragraph is translated from the abstract of (
              %a{:href => "#BeilinsonLectures2014"}> Beilinson’s Yaroslavly’ lectures
              ).
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            %em p-adic Hodge theory
            is the study of properties of p-adic (étale, de Rham, logarithmic crystalline)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            (and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+spaces"} analytic spaces
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %ul
            %li
              %p p-adic de Rham cohomology equipped with the Hodge filtration,
            %li
              %p log-crystalline cohomology (of a potentially semi-stable model) equipped with its Frobenius and monodromy map,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-%C3%A9tale+cohomology"} pro-étale cohomology
                of the extension of the given variety to a
                %code chosen
                algebraic closure of the base field, together with its natural action of the Galois group.
          %p There is an “evident” isomorphism between p-adic de Rham cohomology and log-crystalline cohomology (both are given by a kind of differential calculus).
          %p The main theorem of p-adic Hodge theory is that the datum of these two cohomologies and their relation on the one side, and of pro-étale cohomology on the other side, mutually determine each other (once equipped with their natural additional structures).
          %p
            This result has been conjectured first by Grothendieck in the case of p-divisible groups. He called it the
            = succeed "." do
              %code mysterious functor
            %span.newWikiWord
              p-adic periods
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/p-adic+periods"} ?
            by Fontaine. After these important Breakthrough, the theory was fully developed by a long list of contributors (see
            = succeed "," do
              %code periodes p-adiques
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+mathematics"} almost mathematics
          %p During the last few years, there were interesting new developments.
          %p
            If one works with rational coefficients (no torsion), one may get the isomorphism between both structures in a geometric way (in an algebraic setting), using Beilinson’s approach, through (derived de Rham cohomology)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"}> de Rham complex
            \.
          %p
            It is also possible to use Scholze’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfectoid+spaces"} perfectoid spaces
            and Faltings’
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+mathematics"} almost mathematics
            to give a very elegant proof of this theorem for proper (or more generally, semi-stably compactified) p-adic (strict, i.e., rigid) analytic varieties. The fact that the classical result extends to the analytic setting is essentially due (up to difficulties related to the resolution in finite characteristic, that are dealt with using Faltings’ methods) to the fact that (proper) rigid analytic varieties always have an integral model, given by a formal scheme over the ring of integers of the given non-archimedean field. The same proof may also extend to the non-strict situation by using a convenient base extension, but this work has not yet been done.
          %p The very important case of torsion coefficients has also been addressed by Scholze in his setting, and by Bhatt, who gave a useful refinement of Beilinson’s construction. Remark that almost mathematical tools seem to be quite well adapted to the study of completed integral cohomology.
          %p
            Scholze’s original method involves the use of Witt vectors (already used in Fontaine’s definition of period rings), that were also studied by Kedlaya and Liu in this Hodge theoretic context. This Witt vector approach are interesting, but they seem to have the drawback of being quite hard to globalize (including the archimedean norm in a context of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+Hodge+theory"} global Hodge theory
          %p
            It is quite clear to the experts that a nice way to overcome the difficulties that appear when one uses Witt vectors would be to combine directly the (analytic)
            %code derived de Rham
            approach of Beilinson and Bhatt to the
            %code perfectoid and almost mathematics
            approach of Faltings and Scholze. Both approaches may be extended to the analytic setting using overconvergent derived analytic spaces (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
          %p One may say that almost geometrical derived methods could be useful to study integral completed cohomology, while usual derived geometric methods are quite well adapted to the study of torsion phenomena in finite characteristic.
          %p
            Indeed, as explained by Bhatt at the end of his paper, one may use derived de Rham cohomology over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Z
            to get a (non-archimedean) period ring isomorphism for an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+variety"} arithmetic variety
          %h2#hodgetate_decomposition Hodge-Tate decomposition
          %p
            An early result in
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a smooth projective variety over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_{p}
            (or more generally some finite extension of it). Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}_{p}
            be the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+complex+numbers"} p-adic complex numbers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi ℚ
